arXiv:2504.20196v1 [cs.SE] 28 Apr 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Daye Nam, Ahmed Omran, Ambar Murillo, Saksham Thakur, Abner Araujo, Marcel Blistein, Alexander Frömmgen, Vincent Hellendoorn, Satish Chandra
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Google</span>, <span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">USA</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">© none</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) are rapidly transforming software engineering, with coding assistants embedded in an IDE becoming increasingly prevalent. While research has focused on improving the tools and understanding developer perceptions, a critical gap exists in understanding how developers actually use these tools in their daily workflows, and, crucially, where they struggle. This paper addresses part of this gap through a multi-phased investigation of developer interactions with an LLM-powered code editing and transformation feature—Transform Code—in an IDE widely used at Google. First, we analyze telemetry logs of the feature usage, revealing that frequent re-prompting can be an indicator of developer struggles with using Transform Code. Second, we conduct a qualitative analysis of unsatisfactory requests, identifying five key categories of information often missing from developer prompts. Finally, based on these findings, we propose and evaluate a tool—AutoPrompter—for automatically improving prompts by inferring missing information from the surrounding code context, leading to a 27% improvement in edit correctness on our test set.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the first major win for the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) in coding came from autocomplete <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">cop, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, where the cursor position and the surrounding code were the main inputs to an LLM, the emphasis has shifted to situations in which some amount of developer input – or <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prompt</span> — is expected to convey the intent to the LLM. This holds, whether it is for code generation, or editing/transformation of existing code, or indeed as a part of a multi-step interaction between the programmer and the LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A notable difference between the autocomplete and prompt-based features is the need for developers to write the prompts.
To receive the desired output from such tools, developers need to clearly elaborate their needs, which can require both time and cognitive effort.
As much previous research in understanding professional developers’ perceptions and productivity gains was conducted with autocomplete as the main focus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paradis et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, we still have unanswered questions on developers’ prompting strategies for code editing tools.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transform Code.</span>
We specifically focus on the case of explicitly prompted code editing or transformation, and conduct a case study of Transform Code (<a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Transform Code is a feature in internal IDE at Google, powered by an LLM trained on many popular programming languages, wherein developers can modify and extend code with custom natural language prompts <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in context</span> of a selection of code within the editor.
When a developer selects a certain range of code and activates the feature using a shortcut or by clicking a button, a small Transform Code window pops up where the developer can enter a short natural language prompt indicating the edit or transformation that they would like to see applied to that code.
Once the LLM provides a response, the suggested code edit is displayed as a diff in the original code, and the developer can either accept or reject it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows a request by a developer to <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“simplify”</span> a piece of code (a), who and receives a suggested code edit that simplifies the highlighted code (b).
However, often times, the suggested code edits developers receive can be different from what they expected. In <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for example, the suggested code edit changes the functionality to simplify it, which is likely not the developer’s intention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The above use of prompts, however, is different from that in prompt-driven code generation that is now popular with modern LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roziere et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; the specific use of prompts here is for in-IDE local code transformations.
The prompts are typically short, a single sentence of around 15 words, but typically even shorter.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2504.20196v1/images/tc-before-old.png" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/163;" width="476" height="163" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A developer activates Transform Code using a shortcut or by clicking a button. A Transform Code prompting window pops up, where the developer can input their prompts.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2504.20196v1/images/tc-after-bad-clipped2.png" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/169;" width="476" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Once the LLM provides a response, the code edit is displayed as diff in the developer’s original code. The developer can then either Accept or Discard the suggested code edits (using the first two buttons from the left), or retry the prompt (third button from left).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Transform Code, an LLM-based code editing tool</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We aim to understand the prompting behavior of developers when using Transform Code through a multi-phased study of Transform Code logs. Do prompts generally work as expected? If not, how do people react to unsuccessful prompts. For instance, do people try the same prompt multiple times? Do they alter their prompts and retry?
Finally, how might we assist developers in providing prompts that will produce suggested code edits that align better with their expectations?</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S1.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example of prompt enhancement and the improved Transform Code code edits. <span id="S1.T1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#AAFFAA;">Green</span> indicates the added lines, <span id="S1.T1.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFAAAA;">red</span> indicates the deleted lines, and <span id="S1.T1.7.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;">yellow</span> indicates the changed lines.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S1.T1.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S1.T1.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.8.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S1.T1.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Desired code edit extracted from the future code snapshot</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S1.T1.8.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.8.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S1.T1.8.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block" style="width:165.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.8.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_python ltx_lstlisting ltx_minipage ltx_align_top ltx_listing" style="width:345.0pt;"><span class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></span>
<span id="lstnumberx1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">hosts</span><span id="lstnumberx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">=</span><span id="lstnumberx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">[</span><span id="lstnumberx1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFAAAA;">]</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx2" class="ltx_listingline">
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">for</span><span id="lstnumberx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">host</span><span id="lstnumberx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">in</span><span id="lstnumberx3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">existing_hosts</span><span id="lstnumberx3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFAAAA;">:</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx4" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFAAAA;">name = _HOST_RE.search(host).group(1)</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx5" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFF00;">ip =</span><span id="lstnumberx5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">_HOSTNAME_RE</span><span id="lstnumberx5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">search</span><span id="lstnumberx5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">(</span><span id="lstnumberx5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">host</span><span id="lstnumberx5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">).</span><span id="lstnumberx5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">group</span><span id="lstnumberx5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">(1)</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx6" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFAAAA;">if name not in new_hosts and ip not in new_ips:</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx7" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">        </span><span id="lstnumberx7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFAAAA;">hosts.append(host)</span>
</span>
</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.8.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S1.T1.8.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block" style="width:165.6pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.8.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_python ltx_lstlisting ltx_minipage ltx_align_top ltx_listing" style="width:345.0pt;"><span class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></span>
<span id="lstnumberx8" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">hosts</span><span id="lstnumberx8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">=</span><span id="lstnumberx8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">[</span>
</span>
