<span id="lstnumberx36" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx36.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#AAFFAA;">hosts = [host for host in existing_hosts</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx37" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx37.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">        </span><span id="lstnumberx37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#AAFFAA;">if _HOST_RE.search(host).group(1) not in new_hosts</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx38" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx38.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">        </span><span id="lstnumberx38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#AAFFAA;">and _HOSTNAME_RE.search(host).group(1) not in new_ips]</span>
</span>
</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">First, in <a href="#S3" title="3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we carry out, to our knowledge, the first <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">log-based</span> study of developers’ behavior when it comes to prompting for code transformations.
Previous studies in the area of user experience with AI-based tools have focused on asking developers how they feel through surveys or interview studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">D’Angelo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or through exploratory user studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaithilingam et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barke et al., 2023</a>)</cite> that try to elicit user opinions on the usefulness of AI-based coding tools and on the trust developers place in them.
Since we have extensive fine-grained logs of developer interactions with this AI-based feature in a widely used IDE at a large company, we have the opportunity to make behavioral observations.
For instance, we can tell how often, and to what extent, developers tend to undo and repeat the exact same prompt, and to what extent they accept an AI-based suggested code edit only to heavily change it afterwards.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we approximate the “ground truth” answer expected by the developer,
by looking at a future code snapshot or developer-submitted feedback for the code suggestion.
This allows us to as ask, <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">counterfactually</span>, what would have been a prompt that produced that ground truth answer, or something very close to it.
From a semi-automated study that enhances user prompts by given the ground truth edits,
we find that 59.72% of the unsatisfactory code edits could be improved to be semantically equivalent to the ground truth.
We also conduct a qualitative study to understand the commonly missing or under-specified details in the developer prompts by analyzing the differences between the original and the improved prompts.
We find five common types of information missing from prompts: specifics, operationalization plan, localization/scope, codebase context, and developer intent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, rather than ask developers to work harder, we propose tool support for <span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">automatic prompt enhancement</span> in <a href="#S6" title="6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Here,
the desired code edit is not known, but based on the categories of commonly missing or under-specified elements in developer prompts from the above study, we automatically augment—using an LLM—a prompt to append it with (likely) relevant contextual information to it.
We find that this tool—called AutoPrompter—can improve the effectiveness of developer prompts – which are generally lacking in specificity and context – by around 27%, automatically and without further developer input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivating example.</span>
<a href="#S1.T1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows—for the same example as used in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>—the original developer prompt, a prompt improved by AutoPrompter, the code edits generated by Transform Code using the two prompts, as well as the desired code edit.
The original developer prompt, <span id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“simplify this”</span> neither clearly indicates the developer’s goal, which is to simplify the code to be <span id="S1.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">more concise</span> (i.e., user intent), nor specifies how to simplify the code using Python’s built-in <span id="S1.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">list comprehension</span> (i.e., operationalization plan), based on the desired code edit.
This led Transform Code to originally produce simplified code, but by changing the behavior of the code.
By identifying these missing specifications and reasoning based on the code context provided, AutoPrompter produced a prompt that makes the code more concise using list comprehension, while keeping the functionality intact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Summary of results.</span></p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Around 7% of the Transform Code requests are identical re-prompting requests; moreover, even when developers edit their prompts at the same location, many do not make significant changes. This indicates that developers are reluctant to invest too much work in revising their prompts in the context of Transform Code. [See <a href="#S3" title="3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>]</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our qualitative analysis constructs a codebook of commonly missing information from developers’ prompts: not being specific, missing an operationalization plan, faulty localization/scope, missing code base context or intent. This is done by automatically extracting ground-truth edit information from historical logs and code repository state. [See <a href="#S4" title="4. Real-world Code Editing Data ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5" title="5. Understanding Common Issues of Developer Prompts ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>]</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is possible to automatically enhance the developer’s prompt for Transform Code without further manual input: about 27% of the unsatisfactory code edits in the test set were improved by automatically updating the prompt with AutoPrompter. [See <a href="#S6" title="6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>]</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background &amp; Related work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the research in LLM prompting is extensive, we only touch upon the most pertinent pieces of work in AI developer tools and prompting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>AI Developer Tools for Professional Developers</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, driven by significant improvements in large language models (LLMs), many developer tools have been built upon or have incorporated LLMs.
GitHub Copilot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">cop, 2025</a>)</cite> is one of the earliest LLM-powered developer tools, suggesting code in real time, based on context.
More similar tools have then built, such as Cursor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">cur, 2025</a>)</cite>, Windsurf <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">win, 2025</a>)</cite>, and Bolt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">bol, 2025</a>)</cite>, supporting code completion, editing, and generation, or even agent-based support.
Significant effort has also been invested in AI-enhanced software development tools for internal use, such as at Meta <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bader et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and Google <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandra and Tabachnyk, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tabachnyk, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Froemmgen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, to support their developers’ workflows and proprietary codebases.
Many have conducted studies to understand their potential to enhance developer productivity, investigating both <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">perceived</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bird et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murillo et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actual</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paradis et al., 2024</a>)</cite> productivity gains, through various methodologies.
Research has also studied challenges developers face when using AI tools.
A notable struggle reported multiple times is in effectively communicating requirements to AI tools, which often requires developer cognitive effort <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parameswaran et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaithilingam et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tafreshipour et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Analyzing telemetry logs to understand usage patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murphy-Hill et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and improve features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bibaev et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Froemmgen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a common practice in software engineering.
However, AI tools have only recently been broadly adopted by developers for real-world usage and are rapidly evolving.
Consequently, research on how developers incorporate these tools into their actual workflows has been limited.
More recently, some efforts have been made to measure the overall impact of AI tools at scale, such as by measuring acceptance rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2022</a>)</cite> or the number of pull requests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
However, these studies primarily focused on quantifying productivity gains rather than understanding <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> developers use the tools.
One notable exception is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mozannar et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which collected and analyzed telemetry data from programmers using Copilot.
While that paper provides valuable insights into programmer activities using telemetry segments, the data was collected from only 21 participants and the analysis was done after manual labeling.
In contrast, our analysis examines logs from developers at Google, reporting insights at a large scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Existing Approaches for Prompt Enhancement</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As early research has identified challenges in writing effective prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parameswaran et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaithilingam et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, researchers and practitioners have explored ways to assist developers in prompt writing.
Several (semi-) automatic approaches have been proposed, e.g., meta-prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fernando et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for automatically optimizing prompts in a systematic fashion.
As another example, it was demonstrated in Automatic Prompt Engineer (APE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> that LLMs can discover human-level prompts through a search process, outperforming manually written prompts on multiple benchmarks.
However, the main research efforts have focused on optimizing the system instruction prompt that can be used for several distinct tasks within the same category, often requiring many examples to <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">automatically</span> quantify the quality gains from prompt improvement.
Another notable example are prompt augmentation tools for image generation, like LMOps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which aim at generating diverse images through augmented developer prompts.
However, their main goal is to generate various outputs, rather than satisfying more constraints by inferring missing specifications from the surrounding context.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Transform Code Log Analysis</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transform Code has been released to Google’s developers for about a year, and has since been adopted by a large fraction of developers, who have varying degrees of expertise with ML.
Through iterative improvements to both the underlying LLM and the user interface, Transform Code has become a core part of developers’ workflows.
Despite its positive reception and appreciation, generating code edits from natural language prompts remains a relatively new concept for developers.
Thus, some developers have expressed challenges, mirroring the experiences of users of similar tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kruse et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the ideal case, a developer using Transform Code would send a request with a prompt for a given task, Transform Code would provide an appropriate code edit, the developer would review the edit and proceed to the next task. However, in practice, it is unrealistic to expect an LLM-powered tool to provide the perfect solution on the first attempt.
In this section, we analyze telemetry logs from Transform Code and the IDE, focusing on how developers use Transform Code, and how they react when Transform Code does not provide the ideal code edit they anticipated.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand developer struggles and think of possible remedies, we have to start with data. Here is the data that we have available based on actual usage of Transform Code.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1. </span>In-IDE Event Logs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collected one month of user telemetry logs from the internal Google IDE, from January 27, 2025, to February 23, 2025.
The telemetry data, which is collected automatically, contains in-IDE events ranging from feature usage (e.g., Transform Code) to basic actions (e.g., pressing the Enter key), along with timestamps for each event.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2. </span>Transform Code Logs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Logs for specific features covering non-IDE aspects, e.g., Transform Code prompts, are collected separately at Google.
Transform Code-specific telemetry logs capture developer interactions with Transform Code, including timestamps, prompts, the context in which Transform Code was triggered, any code highlighted by the developer, and the generated Transform Code edits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3. </span>Dataset used for analysis</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From this combined dataset, we sampled data of individual developers who used Transform Code at least 24 times during the month, which is the median usage of the entire population.
This threshold was set to minimize noise arising from non-regular usage of Transform Code, e.g., where new users might test Transform Code with simple prompts like <span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"hi."</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.4. </span>Limitations</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The IDE was instrumented to log an event whenever developers performed any edit, along with the source of this edit (e.g., copy/pasting, typing, running a code formatter, etc.).
Due to the number of possibilities for how developers can perform edits in the IDE, a small percentage of edits (&lt;1%) do not have any source assigned to them.
Many data points were also excluded during data preprocessing, for example, when joining In-IDE event logs and Transform Code logs, as the logs are collected at different granularities.
However, given the large size of the data, we believe it still shows the general workflow when Transform Code is available.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20196v1/reject-self.svg" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:357/307;" width="357" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">After rejection.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20196v1/accept-self.svg" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:357/288;" width="357" height="288"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">After acceptance.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">In-IDE developer action transition graphs. Each node displays an IDE action, and each edge indicates the proportion of transitions between the connected actions. Blue arrows show the first action taken right after a Transform Code request, and gray arrows indicate the second action following the first. For space and readability reasons, edges representing transition frequencies lower than 3% (for the first action) and 10% (for the second action) have been omitted.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Post-Transform Code interactions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To first understand how developers interact with Transform Code in real-world tasks, we focused on the in-IDE actions developers take after their Transform Code requests.
We collected the subsequent in-IDE developer actions after requesting Transform Code and either accepting or rejecting a response.
The challenge here is to accurately associate whether a certain action is a reaction to another, as we cannot reliably determine the developer’s intent without asking them.
Thus, as a best approximation, we only included the Transform Code requests and the subsequent in-IDE actions, when they happened within a short window, 10 minutes, and when the subsequent in-IDE actions were made in the same parts of the code.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1.4. Limitations ‣ 3.1. Data ‣ 3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> visualizes the two consecutive in-IDE developer actions after requesting Transform Code.
We first analyzed the actions made after rejection, to understand how developers cope with unsatisfactory Transform Code edits.
Then, we also analyzed the actions made after acceptance, <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1.4. Limitations ‣ 3.1. Data ‣ 3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-(a), to check for any differences in developers’ reactions.
This distinction is also important because the Transform Code edit is applied to the user’s code upon acceptance, but discarded upon rejection.
Thus, subsequent edits made by the user in the same parts of the code after acceptance likely indicate refinement or adjustment of the applied Transform Code edit, whereas actions taken after rejection indicate the user is continuing to work on the original code.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span>Post-rejection</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most common subsequent action after rejecting a Transform Code edit was Type (33.5%), including typing a character or inserting a line.
This indicates that developers often start working on their tasks by themselves after checking an unsatisfactory Transform Code edit.
A particularly interesting next action was a repeated request to Transform Code (24.3%), which is the second most common action after rejecting a Transform Code edit.
This likely indicates that developers re-prompted Transform Code for the same task.
We found that not an insignificant proportion of developers (22.2%) made even another Transform Code request, possibly indicating developer struggle in using Transform Code, and/or their desire to delegate the task to Transform Code instead of picking up on the task themselves, like those who started typing.
The following frequent subsequent actions were Delete (20.1%), Paste (9.2%) and Cut (3.1%), showing other mechanisms of editing the original code, possibly adopting code from code or documentation search.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2. </span>Post-acceptance</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most common subsequent action was Delete (32.9%) and the second most frequent action was Type (28.7%).
This indicates that the developers frequently make edits <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">to</span> the Transform Code-edited code, after accepting a response, to further refine it.
The next most frequent action was a repeated request to Transform Code (11.9%).
Even after accepting the Transform Code edit, users requested Transform Code from the same code region more than 10% of the time.
This could indicate that developers found unsatisfactory edits <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after</span> accepting the edits, and re-prompted for the same task, or it is still possible that developers moved on to the next task and requested another code edit within the same part (e.g., adding documentation after implementing a function).
Developers also made edits via other mechanisms, possibly to the Transform Code-edited code, by pasting code (5.8%), or cutting the code (5.1%).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Characteristics of Re-prompting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By analyzing the post-Transform Code interactions, we found that developers often request Transform Code again without making any changes to the code (<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">re-prompting</span>).
This either indicates that Transform Code provided a code edit that perfectly addressed the user’s request and the user moved on to the next task, or, the suggested code edit was unsatisfactory and the user made a follow-up request.
To understand this signal better, we conducted a more focused analysis on consecutive Transform Code requests.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>Frequency of Identical Re-prompting</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first investigated how often developers re-prompt with an identical prompt.
We started with identical re-prompting, as it is very likely that the user is making a follow-up request, instead of moving on the next task after getting a satisfactory response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this analysis, we considered a request as an identical re-prompting, when it uses the identical prompt as the previous request and the request is made within 10 minutes after the previous request.
As one may use the same prompt for different code locations for different tasks – for example, one can use <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“write javadoc”</span> for different functions, which indicates that the user is satisfied with the Transform Code edits – we also excluded cases where the code selected for the request (e.g., highlighted code in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (a)) was distant (not within 3 lines).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Out of a set of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>100,000 Transform Code requests, we found that roughly 7% of requests repeated the previous prompt.
There were even some (rare) cases where a developer re-prompted Transform Code with the identical prompt up to 13 times.
This indicates that a significant portion of consecutive Transform Code requests are indeed follow-up requests for an unsatisfying code edit, rather than developers moving on to the next task after receiving a satisfactory code edit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Re-prompting with tweaks</h4>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2504.20196v1/images/tweak-ned.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/157;" width="476" height="157" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2504.20196v1/images/tweak-ned-sametask.png" id="S3.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/163;" width="476" height="163" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F3.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2504.20196v1/images/tweak-ed-sametask200.png" id="S3.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/163;" width="476" height="163" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of the normalized edit distances between two consecutive prompts of the entire data, including prompts written for different tasks (top), and distributions of the normalized edit distances (middle) and the edit distances (bottom) between two consecutive prompts for the <span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">same task</span>. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After confirming that re-prompting behavior may signal an unsatisfactory Transform Code edit, we checked whether developers make efforts to receive a better Transform Code edit, by tweaking their prompts.
Similar to the identical re-prompting data collection, we considered a request as a re-prompting, if the request was within 10 minutes after the previous request, and the selected code for the requests was similar.
Then, to measure the differences between the two prompts, we computed the Levenshtein distance, the minimum number of characters to edit to change one string into the other, and the normalized Levenshtein distance by dividing it by the maximum length of the two prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.3.2. Re-prompting with tweaks ‣ 3.3. Characteristics of Re-prompting ‣ 3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> displays the result.
Out of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>108000 pairs of prompts, we found <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>22000 (20%) were identical re-prompts.
However, as it is possible that the two prompts are for different requests, such as [<span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“rename this”</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“add null check”</span>], aside from the identical re-prompts, the edit distances cannot be directly interpreted.
Thus, we manually investigated <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>130 pairs of prompts that were requested from the same code region, and found that around 50% of them were written for different tasks.
<a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.3.2. Re-prompting with tweaks ‣ 3.3. Characteristics of Re-prompting ‣ 3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-middle and -bottom display the distributions of edit distances for the same task after excluding the 50% of pairs written for different tasks.
The skewed distributions of edit distance show that developers rarely update their prompts when re-prompting, and tend to make small edits.
This is an impediment to using Transform Code most effectively, because the quality of prompts significantly influences the quality of the LLM response.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we observed that developers often do not invest sufficient time in crafting effective prompts when using Transform Code.
Although documentation and the Transform Code user interface instructed users to provide specific details and necessary context, we found that few users did so when tweaking the prompt.
Developers typically resorted to manual editing or invested minimal effort in refining the prompt for a subsequent attempt when an initial, low-effort attempt with Transform Code yielded unsatisfactory code edits.
This behavior suggests that there may be an ‘economics of prompting,’ where developers weigh the costs of prompting against the expected benefits.
Thus, to improve the user experience and maximize the benefits of Transform Code, enhancing the underlying model performance alone is not sufficient; efforts must also focus on reducing users’ cognitive load during prompting and on helping them write prompts more effectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Real-world Code Editing Data</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To inform the development of solutions that help developers write more effective prompts for Transform Code, our initial objective was to understand current prompting practices and identify areas for improvement.
Towards this goal, we collected a dataset of real-world Transform Code usage, specifically focusing on developer requests that yielded <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> satisfactory outcomes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>User Submitted Desired Code Edits</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transform Code has a feedback mechanism, where developers unhappy with the code edit suggestion from Transform Code can submit their desired code edit instead. This improved suggestion is bundled with the original prompt, code context, and original Transform Code suggested code edit upon submission.
For these examples, the desired code edits most probably satisfy the user intent and expectations, as developers themselves need to deliberately create and submit the improved rewrite.
However, not all developers are motivated to invest time in submitting such rewrites; therefore, the number of these examples are quite limited.
After excluding the examples used for the fine-tuning of the Transform Code model, we had 52 examples left for this analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Automatic Extraction of Desired Code Edits</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To collect more examples for the analysis, we used the Transform Code telemetry logs (see <a href="#S3" title="3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) to generate a dataset containing user requests that resulted in unsatisfactory code edits, along with the desired code edit.
As found in the previous section, we used identical re-prompting as an indicator of user dissatisfaction with the initial code edits suggested by Transform Code.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20196v1/extraction-apr24.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Developers make changes to the code (see <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Post-Transform Code interactions ‣ 3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) to fully resolve the task described in the prompt for the request, regardless of how (un)satisfied they are with the Transform Code edits are. To obtain the ground-truth code edit given the user request to Transform Code, we extract a future snapshot of the code after the makes these edits.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain the desired code edit, we extracted a future snapshot of the code, assuming that regardless of how (un)satisfied the developers were, the task would have been resolved at some later point in time (see <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.2. Automatic Extraction of Desired Code Edits ‣ 4. Real-world Code Editing Data ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
We consider the code state after 30 minutes, to leave enough time for developers to make their desired edits, especially for cases where the task requires finding additional information.
However, given that the edit time can vary significantly, depending on the task complexity and the user expertise, it was possible that code edits not relevant to the original Transform Code request could have been included. Developers could also potentially give up on finishing the task, or work on different tasks in the meantime.
This made manual verification necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After collecting candidate Transform Code generated code edits, paired with the desired code edits, we applied filtering heuristics to make the manual verification process tractable, by excluding examples in which:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the original code (before the request) and the desired code edit are identical (i.e., the user decided not to make the code edit),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the Transform Code generated edit and the desired edit are identical (i.e., the user was satisfied with the Transform Code suggestion),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the difference between the original code and the desired code edit, or the Transform Code generated code edit is too long (the length of the unified diff is longer than 10,000 characters)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">there is no future snapshot (+30 minutes) available, because the user deleted the file, or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the desired code edit only removes code from the original code.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This produced 496 examples, which were reviewed manually by two of the authors.
We further filtered away examples—manually—where the edit included changes unrelated to the intent of the original developer prompt. This resulted in 107 high-quality examples after filtering.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20196v1/architecturev2.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:452/372;" width="452" height="372" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the developer prompt error analysis (purple) and automatic prompt enhancement (green). For error analysis, the LLM was given (1) the original user prompt, (2) the original Transform Code edit, (3) the code context, and (4) the desired code edit, to identify missing context from the original user prompt. For automatic user prompt enhancement, AutoPrompter was instructed with the learned missing context from the error analysis, and given (1) the original user prompt, (2) the original Transform Code edit, and (3) the code context. Then, Transform Code generates the code edit using the improved prompt.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Full Dataset for Analysis and Solution Building</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the data collected from the two sources resulted in 159 examples.
Among these, we used 111 examples (70%) of the dataset for understanding developer prompting practices and issues, and we set aside 48 (30%) to be used for testing the solution we would build based on our gained understanding.
Among these, we excluded examples that request code documentation only or simple renaming, where it is challenging to understand why the original Transform Code code edits were not satisfactory, and there is a subjective component involved (see Table E-Example 3 in Appendix).
This resulted in a training dataset of 72 examples, and a test dataset of 33 examples.
As the data were collected from real-world logs, we cannot share the entire dataset as it contains proprietary code, but we can only report the results and share examples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Understanding Common Issues of Developer Prompts</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the collected real-world dataset, we conducted an error analysis of the developer requests that resulted in unsatisfactory code edits from Transform Code, to understand what would be required in a ‘‘better prompt’’.
We leveraged an LLM<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          in our case, Gemini, but we could have used any of the popular LLMs.</span></span></span> to help us identify–for each example–the missing ingredients in a prompt, given prior knowledge of the complete code context, the prompt and the desired edit.
This is based on a premise that with such a “better prompt”, Transform Code would likely have generated the desired solution.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Error Analysis using an LLM</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To collect the common issues with user prompts (i.e., what is missing from the prompt so that Transform Code gives the desired result),
we presented the LLM with: (1) the original user prompt, (2) the code context, (3) the original Transform Code edit, and (4) the desired code edit. Then we asked the LLM to produce (a) an explanation of what was missing from the original user prompt and (b) an improved prompt.
See <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A System Instructions for Error Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for the full system prompt used.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Feasibility Testing</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a sanity check to test whether an LLM can reason about the issues in the user prompts, we tested whether Transform Code can replicate the desired edits from the LLM-enhanced prompts, when provided with the desired edits to enhance the prompt.
Out of 72 examples, we found that 43 enhanced prompts (59.72%) enabled Transform Code to replicate the desired edits.
These numbers indicate that the LLM is able to pinpoint the issues with the user prompts well, and with all the provided information in the prompt, Transform Code is able to generate satisfactory outcomes for a fairly large number of cases.
At the same time, we found that not every unsatisfactory Transform Code edit is due to an incomplete user prompt.
For example, cases that cannot be easily abstracted into a concise natural language prompt (e.g., adding tens of import files without clear patterns across them) could not be improved by rewriting prompts even after seeing the desired edit.
We also discovered that in some examples, Transform Code provided better code edits than the “desired code edits.”
Here the developer may have accepted the Transform Code suggestion, had they seen it, but as we do not know the developer’s original intent, they were not considered semantically identical.
Finally, the underlying model has limitations; it may not be able to generate the desired code edit even when presented with the perfect prompt.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Qualitative Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We qualitatively analyzed the issues of user prompts for Transform Code extracted from the 72 examples in our dataset, using <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">thematic analysis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Braun and Clarke, 2006</a>)</cite>.
Our qualitative analysis consisted of several phases.
Three authors analyzed the original user prompts and the feedback provided by the LLM, which indicated what information was missing from the original prompt using inductive coding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fereday and Muir-Cochrane, 2006</a>)</cite>.
As we reviewed the data, we generated an initial set of categories to describe the different types of missing context and specifications.
If we could not clearly identify which information was missing from the original prompt, we referred to the code context, the original Transform Code edit, and the desired code edit, which also helped mitigate any errors coming from potential hallucinations.
We also wrote analytic memos during the discussion, including observations of patterns and potential connections among the categories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After analyzing 18 examples (25% of the data), we sorted and consolidated the categories into a codebook (<a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.3. Results ‣ 5. Understanding Common Issues of Developer Prompts ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) with detailed definitions and examples.
We then revisited the 18 examples to ensure consistent code application.
Then, two researchers separately categorized the remaining 54 examples using the codebook.
The first round of separate coding resulted in a Krippendorff’s alpha score of 0.81, which shows good agreement.
Then, the ambiguous or difficult examples the two researchers did not agree on were discussed among the three researchers until agreement was reached.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Results</h3>

