</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T2.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:69.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Codebase context</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T2.5.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:27.6pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.5.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">11</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T2.5.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:224.3pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.5.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt itself did not provide necessary background information for the model to be able to generate the desired code edits, including missing information about the broader system, existing codebase. Developers may have assumed that the model had all the necessary context.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T2.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:69.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.5.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">User intent</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T2.5.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:27.6pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.5.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">12</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.5.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T2.5.6.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:224.3pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.5.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Missing or under-specified user intent, in the prompt it is not clear what the developer’s goal is or what type of code edit they want to achieve</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis resulted in 5 codes of missing or under-specified information in user prompts for Transform Code.
<a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.3. Results ‣ 5. Understanding Common Issues of Developer Prompts ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the codebook <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ando et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, including the code, the frequency of the codes, and the description.
Each example could be labeled with up to 2 codes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Specifics.</span>
This code includes examples that lack specifics for the code edit, such as the variable name to use, the value to assign, the API call to use, or the type to use.
For example, Transform Code produces a code edit that is different from the desired code edit given a user request with the prompt <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“fill result vector”</span>, because <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The prompt did not specify the desired type of the ‘result‘ vector. It only mentioned filling a “result vector,” which is ambiguous.” </span>
By using a prompt with a more complete specification, <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Modify the [function] function to create a [type] named ‘result’.”</span>, Transform Code was able to generate the desired code edit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operationalization Plan.</span>
When there are multiple ways of implementing the same goal, if the user does not specify a particular solution, Transform Code may choose the most likely or probable solution.
For example, given the user prompt <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“replace with read -r -p”</span>, the LLM pointed out that <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The prompt lacked explicit instruction on the desired syntax for assigning the user input to the [variable1] variable. The prompt only specified replacing a hardcoded value with user input.” </span>
With the improved prompt, <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Modify the script to prompt the user for [variables], assigning the input directly to the respective variables using ‘read’.”</span>, Transform Code was able to replicate the desired code edit.
Including the operationalization plan can be critical when a user has a specific code modification in mind.
However, in cases when developers want to make open-ended requests, such as <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“write javadoc”</span>, it might be less important <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> Transform Code implements this change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Localization/Scope.</span>
This code includes cases where the user prompt does not provide sufficient information about <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span>, including the location in the code context (e.g., lines) or scope (e.g., all or just one), the code edits should be made.
Given the user prompt <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“surround values with #”</span>, the LLM commented that <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The prompt should explicitly state that all function calls within the selected lines need to be wrapped,”</span> and when improved as <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Wrap all function calls within the [class] block’s CSS variables with ‘# ‘.”</span>, Transform Code could replicate the desired edit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Codebase Context.</span>
Codebase context refers to cases where the user assumes that Transform Code would know all the background knowledge and details about the codebase they are working on, in cases where Transform Code actually had no access to such information.
This could happen because end users might not have a good understanding of how the underlying model is trained, which data are used, and what additional context us included as part of the request, which may have led the developers to assume a level of contextual awareness that the model does not possess.
For example, the desired code edit that Transform Code was not able to produce given <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“add [library]”</span> was successfully replicated with an improved prompt, <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Add "[path to the library]”</span> as a dependency to the [build] rule.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User Intent.</span>
A frequent example of this category is a user asking Transform Code to perform a rather vague and open-ended task.
For example, for a request with the original user prompt <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“make it simpler”</span>, the LLM pointed out that <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The prompt did not specify the intended behavior when [variable1] is present in [variable2]. The model incorrectly interpreted “make it simpler” as removing the redundant assignment instead of preserving the original conditional logic with the necessary ‘continue‘ statement.”</span>
The improved prompt generated by the LLM, <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Refactor the conditional logic for assigning [variable3] to maintain the original behavior, adding a ’continue’ statement when [variable1] is in [variable2].”,</span> successfully replicated the desired code edit.
When prompts lack user intent, compared to other issues, it becomes extremely difficult for Transform Code to produce the desired code edit, as it almost requires reading the minds of developers.
Thus, if the desired code edit matches the most likely code edit to be made in general, given the code context, Transform Code can produce a good code edit, but if not, it is very challenging to achieve the desired code edit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Satisfactory Prompts.</span>
We identified 5 examples from the dataset where the original user prompts appeared satisfactory without requiring further improvement, at least from the researchers’ perspective.
Because the dataset is a collection of user requests that resulted in unsatisfactory code edits, rather than a collection of inadequate prompts, it is reasonable that some of these unsatisfactory code edits are not attributable to the user prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Feasibility of Automatic Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we conducted a qualitative analysis following the traditional thematic analysis process, as this was the first attempt to understand user prompt issues for LLMs in the context of code editing.
However, this process of extracting insights from usage data could potentially be automated with LLMs, similar to other attempts in conducting qualitative research with LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bano et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, particularly to reduce researcher effort and cost.
To assess the feasibility of such automation, we instructed an LLM to carry out the open coding and codebook construction.
We found the LLM-generated codebook to be largely similar to the one created by researchers (see <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C LLM-generated Codebook ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).
This result suggests the potential for applying automated analysis with low cost to understand user challenges in other tool contexts (e.g., automatic program repair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rondon et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) or domains (e.g., code migration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikolov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Automatic User Prompt Enhancement</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section, we identified categories of common issues with user prompts.
In this section, we tested the feasibility of automatically improving user prompts by exploiting this finding, and tested if some of the missing or under-specified user requirements could be inferred from the code context.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Approach to Prompt Enhancement</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test the feasibility of automatically enhancing developer prompts by inferring the developer intent solely based on the developer prompt and to determine how much automatic prompt enhancement can help Transform Code generate the desired code edit, we built and evaluated AutoPrompter.
Given (1) the original developer prompt, (2) Transform Code suggested edit generated with the original developer prompt, and (3) the code context provided by the developer, AutoPrompter was instructed to check whether the given developer prompt misses or under-specifies any of the key information, and to infer them from the given code context to enhance the prompt.
We also instructed AutoPrompter to declare if it cannot confidently infer an improved prompt because of a lack of information, and ti explicitly request additional context instead of giving the improved prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As the LLM is inferring the missing specifications from the code context, it is very likely that there can be multiple probable ways of further specifying the developer prompt.
For example, when a developer asks to <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“document”</span>, it can be <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“improve documentation to be more concise”</span> (user intent) but also <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“write docstrings for every function”</span> (localization/scope).
To make sure that AutoPrompter generates diverse prompts that can cover multiple possible specification options,
we instructed it to provide missing/unspecified instructions given the categories we found in the previous section as a reference, and to improve prompts considering each of those categories.
See <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D System Instruction for Automatic Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for the full system prompt used for AutoPrompter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Evaluation</h3>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.1. </span>Dataset</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used 33 user requests to Transform Code that resulted in unsatisfactory code edits to evaluate the quality of enhanced code edits.
See <a href="#S4" title="4. Real-world Code Editing Data ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for further details.
We note that the goal of this evaluation is to show the feasibility of automatically improving the developer prompt for programming and to demonstrate how such a tool can be evaluated, rather than claiming that AutoPrompter is the ideal approach for developer prompt enhancement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.2. </span>Metrics</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the quality of code edits, we used multiple metrics:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ChrF (Character-level F-score) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Popovic, 2015</a>)</cite>.</span>
Computes an <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> score based on the proportion of character-level <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-grams.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gestalt pattern matching.</span>
Also known as Ratcliff/Obershelp matching. Recursively computes the lengths of the longest common substrings of two strings and normalizes the length of overlapping regions by the total length of both strings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autorater <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</span>
A complement to text-based similarity scores, that can suffer from syntactic differences; measures the semantic similarity between code edits using an LLM-as-a-judge.
Given the original developer prompt, the AutoPrompter enhanced prompt, and the desired code edits, the LLM evaluates the similarity; i.e., how similar the code edits are to the desired code edit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human evaluation.</span>
Two authors separately evaluated the improved code edits by comparing them to the ground-truths and the original user prompts.
Each example was labeled as 1 when the improved code edit was semantically equivalent to the ground truth considering the original user prompt and the code context, and 0 if not.
After separate evaluation, we found a substantial agreement by achieving a 0.86 Cohen’s Kappa score.
For cases where the two authors did not agree, we averaged the two scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We note that none of the metrics can accurately represent the actual quality of code edit performance, but they are complementary to each other, and the combination should be interpreted altogether.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The other popular text based metrics, such as BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papineni et al., 2002</a>)</cite>, are not suitable for assessing the quality of code edits.
We also considered measuring the CodeBLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2020</a>)</cite> scores for the evaluation.
However, as CodeBLEU is language specific in implementation and given the variety of languages our benchmark contains, we decided that extending the CodeBLEU implementation was not practical.</p>
</div>
