</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.3. </span>Limit Study</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in <a href="#S5.SS1.SSS1" title="5.1.1. Feasibility Testing ‣ 5.1. Error Analysis using an LLM ‣ 5. Understanding Common Issues of Developer Prompts ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.1</span></a>, not every code edit benefits from prompt enhancement.
Recognizing that improvement is not expected for all test set examples, we established a theoretical performance <span id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">limit</span> as a reference in <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3. Results ‣ 6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>.
To determine this limit, similar to the feasibility test (<a href="#S5.SS1.SSS1" title="5.1.1. Feasibility Testing ‣ 5.1. Error Analysis using an LLM ‣ 5. Understanding Common Issues of Developer Prompts ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.1</span></a>), we provided AutoPrompter not only with the regular input but also with the desired final code edit, which ensured AutoPrompter received the maximum possible information.
Although evaluating against the full test set might seem unfair because some examples fall outside the scope of our prompt enhancement approach, we decided to keep them as we believe the current setting represents real-world usage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S6.T3" title="In 6.2.2. Metrics ‣ 6.2. Evaluation ‣ 6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the results.
In interpreting the data, we focused on human evaluation results, as we found that text-based similarity scores
often do not correlate with human evaluation, especially when the differences are small.
Moreover, text-based metrics often give unsatisfactory results in the case of syntactic differences that do not matter semantically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The semantic human evaluation count is 0 by design, as the dataset was built to only include unsatisfactory Transform Code code edits, i.e., semantically different from the desired code edit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1. Approach to Prompt Enhancement ‣ 6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> that we ask the LLM to produce multiple prompt variants.
Given multiple prompts, each one improved with a different strategy, we used different ways of selecting the best improved prompt to use.
In <a href="#S6.T3" title="In 6.2.2. Metrics ‣ 6.2. Evaluation ‣ 6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">self-selection</span>, <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">farthest</span>, and <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llm</span> show the performance of different selection approaches.
First, for (<span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">self-selection</span>), we instructed the AutoPrompter directly to select a prompt that is most likely to elicit the desired code edit.
We also tried choosing a prompt for which Transform Code produces the most distinctive code edit from the original code edit (<span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">farthest</span>).
This is based on the idea that if the developer was not satisfied with the original code edit, the desired code edit will more likely be found within the code edit candidates that are different from the unsatisfactory code edit, than within those similar to the unsatisfactory one.
Finally, we used an LLM to select the best improved Transform Code code edit, given the original developer prompt (<span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llm</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the evaluation demonstrates the feasibility of automatically improving the developer prompt:
9 out of 33 (27%) examples were improved by AutoPrompter, which indicates that it is often feasible to infer missing specifications from the given code context.
This seems like a good result especially when considering 37% as the limit (as computed by the process in  <a href="#S6.SS2.SSS3" title="6.2.3. Limit Study ‣ 6.2. Evaluation ‣ 6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2.3</span></a>) for this test set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recall also from <a href="#S6.SS2.SSS3" title="6.2.3. Limit Study ‣ 6.2. Evaluation ‣ 6. Automatic User Prompt Enhancement ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2.3</span></a> that some of the examples may not be amenable to this process of prompt enhancement; and, finally, Transform Code also has limits in terms of the power of the model itself in interpreting the prompt and carrying out a desired edit.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Threats to Validity</h2>

<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Telemetry logs analysis for Transform Code </h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The use of one month of data may be insufficient for broad generalization, particularly given the rapid evolution of AI tools at Google, and the corresponding adaptation of developers.
Furthermore, updates to the LLMs supporting the AI tools, which occur during tool evolution, might influence developer satisfaction and behavior.
However, we had to tolerate these limitations to balance data collection complexity, privacy considerations, and the scale of analysis.
Given the substantial size of Google’s internal developer base and the widespread adoption of Transform Code among developers over an extended period, we believe that our dataset is representative, but should be interpreted considering the use case, user population, and the scope of features.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Qualitative Analysis for Common Issues in Developer Prompts</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The error analysis is subject to the typical threats to validity encountered in other qualitative analyses.
While analyzing data qualitatively allows us to engage in-depth and distill knowledge from previously unexplored areas, we cannot guarantee that the findings generalize beyond the sampled examples without further validation.
Specifically, because complex and lengthy examples were excluded to facilitate practical manual review, our sample may not cover all possible prompts that are used for code editing with an AI tool.
However, given that Google covers diverse application domains and developers with varying expertise in programming, domain knowledge, and AI tools, we believe the sampled examples reasonably represent diverse use cases of code editing tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Correlating in-IDE and Transform Code events</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We already discussed at length in <a href="#S3" title="3. Transform Code Log Analysis ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> the issues in achieving perfect alignment between IDE logs and Transform Code logs, and this could hinder some precision in our analysis; however, with the scale, we believe this concern is mitigated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Specific to Transform Code </h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments with improving prompt effectiveness have all been in the context of Transform Code. We believe that the ideas will carry over to similar tools where prompts are used to make code edits (e.g. Cursor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">cur, 2025</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Discussion &amp; Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we have studied how we can improve developer prompts so that Transform Code produces code edits that more closely align with developer expectations and intentions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach is to analyze the common reasons—the categories of missing detail—that the prompts themselves could be lacking. We did this by leveraging an LLM and asking it to articulate the prompt enhancements that would be needed to get Transform Code to produce edits that match the given desired edit that each of those prompts was supposed to produce. We collated this information into a codebook that captures five common classes of missing information: missing some specific information, missing an operationalization plan, faulty localization/scope, missing codebase context, or missing intent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This analysis then enabled us to create AutoPrompter, a tool that pro-actively adds details to the prompt (without knowing the ground truth, of course) to get Transform Code to produce a more satisfactory edit from a given developer prompt.
This idea seems to have shown promise, as we show in our test set: 27% of the cases that were not achieving a satisfactory result with Transform Code now accomplished a satisfactory result with the automatically enhanced prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although we focused on testing the feasibility of automatically enhancing user prompts in this paper, there is a lot of room for future work.
First, the identified common issues with user prompts can be used to guide the training strategies of the underlying LLM for Transform Code, such that Transform Code can infer such missing information even with the original, under-specified user prompts.
Using an automated analysis of usage data with LLMs (see <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. Feasibility of Automatic Analysis ‣ 5. Understanding Common Issues of Developer Prompts ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>), this process of learning from user struggles can be generalized to other LLM-powered tools in identifying gaps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Another promising usage of AutoPrompter would be to use it for the automatic curation of training data from the usage logs.
Transform Code usage data can be very useful for training, as it offers negative examples (original Transform Code edit) alongside ground-truths (user "rewrites") given user prompts.
However, only a small, human-verified subset of user-submitted ground truths (see <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. User Submitted Desired Code Edits ‣ 4. Real-world Code Editing Data ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) has been used, due to the challenges in extracting quality ground-truths, discussed in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Automatic Extraction of Desired Code Edits ‣ 4. Real-world Code Editing Data ‣ Prompting LLMs for Code Editing: Struggles and Remedies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
The core idea is to include the AutoPrompter-improved Transform Code edit (edit generated using the AutoPrompter-enhanced user prompt) as ground-truths, <span id="S8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only if</span> they are identical to a future snapshot.
Because AutoPrompter only augments the prompt based on the provided context, the improved Transform Code edit captures the original user intent without adding requirements not mentioned in the original prompt.
The identity to a future code snapshot also shows implicit user confirmation that the improved edit was desired.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, one potential benefit of AutoPrompter, not discussed in this paper as it is out of scope, is the increased transparency and control it offers users.
As an enhanced prompt is generated to obtain an enhanced code edit from Transform Code, when presented, users can easily verify whether their intent is accurately described in the prompt.
This prompt can then be easily updated by the user if it contains errors, affording them direct control over the input to Transform Code.
In addition, presenting these improved prompts to developers could help them learn to write more effective prompts themselves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, possibly enabling them to understand the benefits of adding more context. This understanding, in turn, could lead to better code edits from Transform Code with less accumulated effort.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
